e., the image of $h'$ in $C_e$). Therefore we have a distinguished component $\Pi_0$ of $\Pi$ containing paths $(T_s)$ such that $T_s(B)$ meets $B'$ for all $s$. The transport of $\widetilde T$ along any path from $\Pi_0$ gives the component $\widetilde\Phi$. This concludes the argument.

